// ============================================================================
// Project 25 (Meridian Pay) -- Session 3, Phase 2
// 01b_dispute_curated_FIX.kql             [2026-08-18]
//
// Supersedes the dispute_curated section of 01_update_policies.kql, which
// failed with:
//     Kusto.DataNode.Exceptions.InvalidPolicyOperationException
//     "Request is invalid and cannot be executed."
//
// RUN THESE ONE STATEMENT AT A TIME (click inside a statement, press Run).
// Statements are separated by blank lines so the Queryset picks up exactly one
// per Run.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY IT FAILED -- CORRECTED 2026-08-18, SECOND DIAGNOSIS
//
// FIRST DIAGNOSIS, WRONG: "an update policy query may only read its declared
// Source table, and this one joined auth_curated." Recorded here rather than
// erased, because it was wrong in an instructive way -- it fit the evidence
// available at the time (the two working policies read one table each; this
// one read two), and it was CONFIRMED by nothing. Removing the join did not
// fix the error, which is what disproved it. Kusto update policies CAN read
// other tables.
//
// ACTUAL CAUSE: schema mismatch. The policy's query returns 6 columns; the
// target table still has 9, because 01_update_policies.kql created
// dispute_curated with three extra columns (original_event_time,
// auth_age_at_dispute_days, maturity_cohort) and .create-merge -- used in the
// first version of this fix -- adds columns but NEVER REMOVES them. Kusto
// raises the same InvalidPolicyOperationException for a schema mismatch as for
// an invalid query, which is exactly why the first diagnosis survived as long
// as it did. The lesson is the engine's own: an error code shared by several
// causes is not a diagnosis, and "it fits" is not "it is confirmed."
//
// The redesign below (cohort at query time, not stored) is kept REGARDLESS,
// because it was right on its own merits even though the reason I first gave
// for it was not:
//
//   An update policy fires per ingestion batch, against whatever is in the
//   database AT THAT MOMENT. Stamping maturity_cohort at ingest time freezes
//   an answer that is supposed to move. A dispute ingested before its original
//   auth lands would be written 'unresolved_original_missing' PERMANENTLY --
//   the policy never re-runs to correct it. Worse, cohort is a function of
//   ELAPSED TIME: a dispute that is 'partially_matured_T60_T30' today becomes
//   'graded_T90_T60' thirty days later. A stored column cannot express that.
//   It would have gone stale silently and shown a confidently wrong number on
//   the Alert Precision page -- exactly the defect contract Sec5 exists to
//   prevent ("reporting precision across an ungraded cohort is the defect this
//   design exists to avoid").
//
// So cohort moves from a STORED COLUMN to a QUERY-TIME FUNCTION, which is
// always correct because it reads the data as it stands when asked. This does
// not weaken the project's structural claim -- component 4 is "KQL functions:
// the hot path's measures", and this is precisely that. The transformation
// layer is still update policies + materialized views + functions, with no
// Spark notebook anywhere near it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---- STEP 1 (DIAGNOSTIC FIRST -- run this and READ IT) --------------------
// Rev 1 of this file used .drop + .create-merge here, and step 4 STILL failed
// with InvalidPolicyOperationException. Reason: .create-merge NEVER REMOVES
// COLUMNS. If the drop did not take effect, dispute_curated still carries the
// three columns the old design added (original_event_time,
// auth_age_at_dispute_days, maturity_cohort) -- so the table has 9 columns
// while the policy's query returns 6. An update policy whose query output does
// not match its target table's schema EXACTLY is rejected, and the error Kusto
// raises for it is InvalidPolicyOperationException -- the same error as the
// cross-table-join problem, which is what made the first diagnosis wrong.
//
// Confirm the actual shape before changing anything:

dispute_curated | getschema | project ColumnName, ColumnType

// EXPECT exactly 6 rows: dispute_id, auth_id, dispute_time, dispute_reason,
// dispute_outcome, amount.
// If you see 9 (the three extra columns above), that is the cause -- continue
// to step 2. If you genuinely see 6, STOP: the cause is something else and
// this fix will not help.

// ---- STEP 2: force the exact schema ---------------------------------------
// .alter table (NOT .create-merge) sets the schema to exactly this list,
// REMOVING any column not named here. That is what makes it the right tool
// and .create-merge the wrong one. Safe: the table holds no rows, because its
// policy never attached, so there is no data to lose.

.alter table dispute_curated (
    dispute_id: string,
    auth_id: string,
    dispute_time: datetime,
    dispute_reason: string,
    dispute_outcome: string,
    amount: real
)

// Re-check before moving on -- must now be 6 columns:
//   dispute_curated | getschema | project ColumnName, ColumnType

// ---- STEP 3: the transform, single-source ---------------------------------

.create-or-alter function fn_transform_dispute_curated() {
    raw_dispute_events
    | summarize arg_max(dispute_time, *) by dispute_id
    | project dispute_id, auth_id, dispute_time, dispute_reason,
              dispute_outcome, amount
}

// ---- STEP 4: attach the policy --------------------------------------------

.alter table dispute_curated policy update @'[{"IsEnabled":true,"Source":"raw_dispute_events","Query":"fn_transform_dispute_curated()","IsTransactional":false,"PropagateIngestionProperties":false}]'

// ---- STEP 5: the cohort logic, as a query-time function -------------------
// This is counted function 11 (it replaces fn_maturity_cohort in the 12 --
// see the note at the end of this file). Pass as_of = datetime(2026-08-17)
// when querying backfill data, because the generator's clock is frozen at
// SIM_NOW and real now() drifts past it.
//
// Returns one row per dispute with its original auth's event_time, the auth's
// age when disputed, and BOTH cohorts:
//   cohort_at_dispute -- how mature the auth was when the dispute was raised
//   cohort_now        -- how mature that auth is as of as_of. THIS is the one
//                        the Alert Precision page groups by, and the one that
//                        a stored column could never have kept current.

.create-or-alter function fn_dispute_graded(as_of: datetime = datetime(null)) {
    let ref_time = iff(isnull(as_of), now(), as_of);
    dispute_curated
    | join kind=leftouter (
        auth_curated
        | where event_type == 'auth'
        | project auth_id, original_event_time = event_time, terminal_id,
                  store_id, merchant_id, issuer_bin, auth_amount = amount
      ) on auth_id
    | extend age_at_dispute_days = todouble(datetime_diff('day', dispute_time, original_event_time))
    | extend age_now_days = todouble(datetime_diff('day', ref_time, original_event_time))
    | extend cohort_at_dispute = case(
        isnull(original_event_time), 'unresolved_original_missing',
        age_at_dispute_days >= 60.0, 'graded_T90_T60',
        age_at_dispute_days >= 30.0, 'partially_matured_T60_T30',
        'awaiting_grading_T30_T')
    | extend cohort_now = case(
        isnull(original_event_time), 'unresolved_original_missing',
        age_now_days >= 60.0, 'graded_T90_T60',
        age_now_days >= 30.0, 'partially_matured_T60_T30',
        'awaiting_grading_T30_T')
    | project dispute_id, auth_id, dispute_time, dispute_reason, dispute_outcome,
              amount, original_event_time, terminal_id, store_id, merchant_id,
              issuer_bin, age_at_dispute_days, age_now_days,
              cohort_at_dispute, cohort_now
}

// ---- STEP 6: verify. Run each separately. ---------------------------------
// Policy attached to all three curated tables (expect 3 rows, IsEnabled=true):
//   .show table auth_curated policy update
//   .show table telemetry_curated policy update
//   .show table dispute_curated policy update
//
// After the backfill lands, the cohort distribution -- this is the number the
// Alert Precision page is built on, so read it early:
//   fn_dispute_graded(datetime(2026-08-17))
//   | summarize disputes = count() by cohort_now
//   | order by disputes desc
//
// EXPECT: no 'unresolved_original_missing' rows. Every one of the 6,715
// disputes references an auth that exists in the 90-day backfill by
// construction (GENERATOR_SPEC.md Sec4.3: dispute_time = auth.event_time +
// Uniform(30,60) days, and disputes are only generated FOR auths that exist).
// If that bucket is non-empty, either the auth ingest is incomplete or the
// join key is wrong -- investigate before trusting any precision number.

// ============================================================================
// FUNCTION COUNT, kept honest.
// The contract commits to 12 KQL functions. This file adds fn_dispute_graded.
// To stay at 12 rather than quietly becoming 13, fn_maturity_cohort (04_*.kql
// #11) is DEMOTED to a helper -- same status as fn_store_open_sessions. It
// remains created and usable as a scalar convenience, but it is no longer one
// of the counted 12, because nothing calls it now that fn_dispute_graded
// returns the cohort directly alongside the row it describes.
//
// The counted 12 are therefore:
//    1-4   fn_reflex1..fn_reflex4                    (04_functions.kql)
//    5-10  fn_dq_rule1..fn_dq_rule6                  (04_functions.kql)
//    11    fn_dispute_graded                         (this file)
//    12    fn_issuers_for_estate_selection           (03_dim_estate_build.kql)
// Uncounted helpers: fn_store_open_sessions, fn_maturity_cohort,
// fn_build_dim_estate, fn_check_zero_orphan_terminals, and the three
// fn_transform_* behind the update policies.
// ============================================================================
